microsimulation, the software SUMO [3], allows to address these issues by supplying detailed models for road user interactions. It would therefore be beneficial, to bring real world interaction data into a form that can be mimicked by the behavior of VRUs in the simulation. This paper aims to provide such models by tackling 2 challenges for improving VRU models: First, a camera solution is used with an internal battery, to be deployed flexibly where road users interact the most and collect video data. That video data can be used to extract trajectories of road user behaviors through machine learning (ML) techniques (see for instance [4]). Secondly, new VRU behavioral models are fit from simulator studies and the video observations and formulate ways to bring real world behaviors into simulation.
2 Methods and Data
2.1 Mobility Observation Box
The mobility observation box (MOB) [5] is a camera solution with an internal power supply and internal encrypted file storage for video data to obtain real world data from ML based analysis of real subject movements. Road users recognized by object detection methods provide the foundation for studying the flows and interactions under everyday conditions (Fig. 1).
Fig. 1. Tracking of the MOB system, discriminating the user type that is identified, at Schwarzenbergplatz in Vienna. Image obtained from the MOB data collection.
Trajectory data was smoothed using linear interpolations on a second long timescale. This study is based on mobility observation box-based trajectory data for a large square in the city of Vienna. Video data immediately provided flows of road users on the square and therefore the necessary material to start a simulation. Data extracted from these interactions includes maximum speeds and accelerations, minimum distance between users, X and Y coordinates of the projected trajectory in the


Extracting Parameters of Vulnerable Road Users’ Behavior 5
2D plane at the point of closest approach, relative angle between trajectories at minimum distance, longitudinal and lateral accelerations (individual and aggregate) during collision courses, TTC and PET values.
2.2 Surrogate Safety Measures
Means to quantify safety relevant interactions come in the form of surrogate safety measures (SSMs) [6]: in particular time to collision (TTC) and post-encroachment time (PET). Time to collision is defined as the time it would take 2 road users to arrive simultaneously at a linear continuation of their current movement, given their current relative speed (i.e., the time until they would be expected to collide unless they change their movement course):
TTC = (Distance to overlapping road user prediction)/(Current relative Speed ) (1)
Post encroachment time is the temporal gap between two road users arriving at the same spot (t1 time road user arrives in overlap area, t0 time other road user arrives in overlap area).
PET = t1 − t0 (2)
For the MOB data, these values were calculated using R [7] based on the collected trajectories. Trajectory data was smoothed via linear interpolation for this, to provide consistent speed values and accelerations.
2.3 Machine Learning Models
Clustering methods were used on the interaction data [8] to obtain a description of subclasses of users behaviors. The implemented technique was k-means, and the optimal number of clusters was determined using the “Elbow method”. This method allows to select the number of clusters from which WCSS (Within-Cluster Sum of Square) decreases in a more progressive way. The results obtained with the Elbow Method have been contrasted and validated by calculating the Silhouette coefficient [9]. Furthermore, a decision tree model [10] was run to extract decision making rules from when an interaction would fall into one cluster or another. The hyperparameters of this model, as well as the performance metrics (accuracy, precision, recall and f1score) were obtained through the k-fold cross validation procedure with 5 iterations. The hyperparameters of the decision tree model are the splitting criterion (cross-entropy, Gini index), maximum depths (None, 3, 5, 10), minimum number of observations to split an internal node (1, 3, 5, 7,10); minimum number of observations in a node (1, 2 or 4).
3 Results
Trajectory data obtained from the MOB was collected and investigated for 3 days of observation (during daylight hours) for a total of 51 h in which road users could be identified. Using our ad-hoc algorithm implemented in R, all road users were found to be on either a collision course with less than or equal 2 s TTC and were in a PET relation with less than 2 s remaining.


