Fig. 2. Clusters extracted from the interactions of VRUs with other road users. Image generated directly from the clustering of the data in RStudio.
The total number of samples is 370 and was best fit using 3 clusters. For these clusters a decision tree analysis was ran as described above. An exemplary outcome for TTC training set can be seen in Fig. 3.
Fig. 3. A decision tree providing different split criteria on when an interaction would fall in one cluster or another.
The decision tree allows to relate the risk classes as a function of the set of variables used in the clustering. The splitting criterion that optimizes the classifier performance is cross-entropy, with a maximum depth of 3 samples, a minimum of 10 observations to split an internal node, and a minimum value of 1 sample to be in a leaf node.


Extracting Parameters of Vulnerable Road Users’ Behavior 7
The accuracy of the model is 88%, with balanced values of average precision and average recall (88% and 81%, respectively), being f1-score 83%. Class 1 encompasses road conflict situations in which there is a higher potential danger. When the speed of the second user is less than 4.1 m/s2, the lateral acceleration is less than 0.52 m/s2, resulting in cases where users exhibit minimal lateral avoidance action. When the speed is high, the aggregate lateral distance is low and the deceleration of the faster user is lower, resulting in a more perilous situation. These findings are supported by the distribution of TTC cases, where cluster 1 collects the majority of the observations for TTC < 2 m/s2 for minimum distance lower than 2 m. Class 2 includes moderate risk situations. Both longitudinal and lateral deceleration are higher, leading to avoidance actions involving a greater minimum distance between users (e.g., stopping, or sidestepping users in front of vehicles entering the intersection or cyclists riding in the bike lane). Finally, Class 3 comprises encounters with greater lateral actions but greater aggregate distances when speed is high. These interactions involve less risk, as the TTC and minimum distance values are higher. These decision rules form a model of road user reactions to collision courses, meaning rules for accelerating or stopping can be extracted directly from experimental data. These rules can be used to model road user behavior in microsimulation software like SUMO, for instance by equipping a simulated VRU with a cluster membership, then choosing their choice parameters (acceleration during collision course, TTC thresholds) during the simulation of the interaction.
4 Discussion
Video data was obtained from a busy square in the city of Vienna and was used to describe interactions on this square. Consequently, a model of road user reactions to collision courses was proposed, to provide more realistic behaviors. Using techniques from machine learning, the resulting interactions were clustered, showing similarities in driven speeds, distances, TTC, PET and accelerations during interaction. A tree-based classification analysis is conducted to provide a number of rules on the interactions, which can in turn be used in simulation to represent road users behaviors. This approach can help assessing reactions of road users (VRUs in particular) to simulated behavioral changes of other road users, such as might be induced by new assistance systems in vehicles. Additionally, this study is a first step of a cycle of development that will require validating the improvements achievable though this modeling approach in SUMO, comparing to further trajectory data and revising the model accordingly. The real-world data helps calibrating models to more situations than an idealized simulator environment can provide. Future work will aim to connect this to earlier simulator work providing behaviors for sudden unexpected collision courses [11], based on reaction time and moving forward or backwards away from the collision area. Limitations include the need to verify on larger data sets, which fortunately can be obtained fairly quickly using the MOB, as well as the need for improvements in object recognition, as data in the traffic environment is commonly affected by problems like partial occlusion or vehicles moving close to one another.


