In Ireland the population has been getting steadily older since the 1980s. In Census 2016, 37.2% were aged 45 and over, compared with 34.4% in 2011 and 27.6% in 1986. Almost a third of the population 33.2% was less than 25 years old, while 29.5% were in the 25–44 age group. The 65 years and over age group saw the largest increase in population since 2011, rising by 102,174 to 637,567, a rise of 19.1% [1]. In February 2020 the Irish Road Safety Authority published the “Road Casualties and Collisions in Ireland 2017 Statistics” which showed that the age cohort of 65 and over were involved in 781 collisions which represents 9.8% of all collisions reported. Inside built-up areas there were 23 vulnerable road users killed while there was 2399 seriously injured which represents 53% and 54% of the total reported incidents. Outside built-up areas there were 21 vulnerable road users killed while there was 215 seriously injured which represents 19% killed and 6% of the total reported incidents [2]. Using the information contained in this report it was concluded that the 65 years and over cohort is represented as a high percentage of overall recorded collisions on the Irish Road Network and that a substantial number of these collisions involved vulnerable road users. Active travel has been highlighted as a priority by many recent policy and strategy documents including the recently published National Strategic Mobility Policy (2022) and the Climate Action Plan (2023). In May 2022 TII launched the National Cycle Network Plan (NCN) which focuses on linking cities and towns of over 5,000 people with a safe, connected and inviting cycle network. It includes plans to create cycle routes to destinations such as transport hubs, centres of education, centres of employment, leisure, and tourist destinations. Where possible, it will optimise the potential for people to cycle as part of their daily activities. The findings of this research will provide clear conclusions and recommendations to inform national standards and the required changes to policy documents were required to ensure equitable mobility for Irelands aging population – referred to in this study as ‘age friendly active travel’. The methodology includes a literature review and targeted survey of the relevant cohort along with in-depth interviews. The research examines all forms of active travel including pedestrians, cyclists, and the use of mobility aids and including personal powered transport.
2 Literature Review
Several studies have explored the ‘lived experiences’ of older people wishing to travel more actively, exploring reasons they avoid it and what they perceive would make it better. In a 2011 study about rural car dependence amongst older people, participants identified safety fears as the key barrier [3]. An Austrian study showed that many older people feel unsafe walking and cycling because of their reduced vision and hearing, especially when sharing roads and paths. In the study, survey participants expressed a clear desire for completely separated active travel paths, and where possible, separation between walking and cycling [4]. A concept originating in the Netherlands is the ‘forgiving cycle path’, or ‘forgiving kerbs’, which seek to address a particular infrastructural issue from the perspective of over 55s. Acknowledging that older people have more trouble with balance on a bicycle, which may lead to them drifting off course and into the opposite lane or in traffic, a


Equitable Mobility - Appropriate Active Travel Facilities 11
‘forgiving’ cycle path or kerb can come in different forms to encourage containment within the cycle lane [5]. Further to this, a study comparing age friendly cycling in the UK and the Netherlands, older people in both countries were shown to be more willing to cycle if routes were completely segregated from traffic. Participants were happy to avoid more direct cycle routes and extend their cycling journeys if cycle paths were completely traffic free and closer to nature, making the experience safer and more enjoyable [6].
2.1 Key Findings from the Literature Review
• Safety and perceived safety amongst older people increase when active travel routes such as cycle lanes or footpaths are completely separate from motor traffic, or at least have a distinct physical barrier. • The quality (and maintenance) of surfaces is also important for older people, reducing the likelihood of trips and falls, which could lead to collisions with other road users. • Design and infrastructure features such as frequent seating, better lighting and distinct surface materials and colours can contribute to the safety of active travel for older people, making routes clearer and more coherent, and allowing opportunities for regular rest. • Active travel links to public transport are especially important for older people in rural areas, who may rely on public transport for longer journeys, but struggle to reach facilities such as bus stops and train stations due to a lack of adequate active travel infrastructure. • Other important factors include reducing speed limits, increasing crossing times on signalised crossings, and introducing low traffic neighbourhoods and modal filters to reduce motor vehicle traffic on active travel routes.
3 Survey Results
414 people aged 55 and over living in Ireland completed an online survey (quantitative stage). 30 of the survey respondents took part in a follow-up depth interview lasting between 30–60 min (qualitative stage). The majority of survey respondents (83%) do journeys by foot once a week or more. Cycling is less common, with 17% using a cycle for journeys at least a few times a month; 72% indicated that they never cycle any journeys. A very small minority of survey respondents use either a wheelchair at least a few times a month (3%) and/or a mobility scooter (2%). Most respondents felt that ‘active travel is good for health and wellbeing’ (98%), and that ‘active travel in enjoyable’ (88%), a much smaller percentage agreed that ‘older people can walk cycle or wheel safely where I live’ (35%). Those who live in rural locations are more likely than those in urban locations to agree that they don’t have the time in their day for active travel and that active travel is not for older people, and less likely to agree that active travel is the norm where they live or older people can travel actively where they live. Very few respondents (14%) travel actively because they have no alternative means of getting to a destination however those who live in rural areas are more likely to give this reason than those living in urban areas.


12 R. Van Ry et al.
Barriers and Safety: Those age 55 to 64 are more likely than those aged 75 or older to be deterred by a lack of separation between cyclists and pedestrians on cycle routes or footpaths and unsafe roads. They are also more likely than all other age groups to be deterred by poor quality surfaces on cycle routes or footpaths and traffic volume/speed. Those who cycle are less likely to report their health and fitness and a preference for traveling by car are barriers to active travel than those who never cycle, and are more likely to report the following barriers:
• Barriers, gates or other infrastructure on cycle routes or footpaths • Poor quality surface on cycle routes or footpaths • Lack of separation between cyclists and pedestrians on cycle routes or footpaths • Traffic volume and/or speed • Unsafe roads, including junctions • Having to transport other people
The most commonly reported factors that would make people feel safer when travelling actively are routes being well-maintained (55%), well-lit (49%) and separated from motorised traffic (45%). Although longer crossing times at pedestrian and cycle crossings is the least common response, this is still an issue for over 30% of survey respondents, and those with a disability in particular.
4 Interview Findings
4.1 Travel Patterns
Cycling: Ten interviewees cycled journeys at least some of the time so there was an opportunity to explore their motivations for using this mode of active travel. Cycling is considered a quicker way of undertaking journeys that are perceived to be too far to walk or that would take too long on foot. Interviewees typically cycle for journeys of ‘a few’ km, with one person cycling up to 10 km. Aside from these ten people, a few others cycle for leisure, recreation, or exercise, but not for purposeful journeys. Some are comfortable cycling for leisure on the roads close to where they live (but would still choose quieter times on the roads where possible), but others prefer to drive to somewhere they can cycle off the road, such as a Greenway. There are definite safety concerns regarding traffic that is limiting the amount of cycling done by interviewees. Those who did not cycle often referred to their safety in traffic as a reason for not travelling in this way.
Walking: All but one of the people interviewed walk at least a few times a month, and most walk once a week or more. Interviewees are very motivated to walk in order to ‘keep moving’ and help them stay healthy, not just physically but mentally too. Particularly for those who are retired, ‘getting out of the house’ regularly is considered important. Those living in rural areas identify fewer opportunities to walk for functional journeys due to the distance between their home and the desired destination. One person had recently moved to a town from a more rural area to be in walking distance of shops


Equitable Mobility - Appropriate Active Travel Facilities 13
and other facilities and so they did not have to rely on driving. Others who live rurally describe travelling to a local big town or city, either by car or public transport, and walking extensively to do what they needed when they were there. A high proportion of the sample are retired and report that they have the time available to walk both for function (rather than having to use the most time-efficient method of transport) and leisure. They also have the flexibility to choose the most appropriate time for them to walk, for example, in order to avoid the bad weather or dark mornings or evenings in the winter. They can meet up with others to walk and socialise at the same time. In contrast, those who are still working typically walk at weekends or during their lunch break.
Car Travel: Those living in rural areas are typically motivated to go by car (either driving themselves or being driven by others) rather than by cycle or on foot by the distance they need to travel to meet certain needs; for example, to get to a ‘large’ supermarket to do a full grocery shop. Those in rural areas also report that there are infrequent or no public transport options to take them where they need to go, so they have to drive.
4.2 Suggested Improvements to Infrastructure
Many suggestions were made by interviewees as to how infrastructure could be improved to enable more older people to travel actively more of the time. A view shared by a number of interviewees is that cyclists and pedestrians are not afforded the same priority as car drivers when it comes to town planning and the development of infrastructure. A few of the suggestions relate more specifically to safer active travel.
Cycling: A lack of cycle routes is a major barrier for people not continuing to cycle into older age – roads are perceived to be too unsafe and so specific or segregated cycling infrastructure is required. Not being able to park a cycle and leave it safely puts people off cycling. Some suggested road changes to enhance the safety of cyclists in traffic; these included an advance green light for cyclists and other changes to at junctions, such as more protected spaces for cyclists.
Walking, Wheeling and Scooting: More footpaths in rural areas in particular could encourage more walking. Other suggested changes include: pedestrian crossings that are more responsive to the needs of the pedestrian rather than the car driver; more dropped kerbs in the correct places for wheelchair and mobility scooter users and people who are not comfortable with steps; improvements in seating, including some with shelters to protect against the weather; wider and better maintained footpaths in order to better accommodate mobility scooters and wheelchairs. Shared use paths are recognised by most people as giving more space to active travelers as a group, but these can also lead to pedestrians feeling vulnerable and unsafe when sharing a space with cyclists. Many felt that more public toilets, shelters and rest stops are essential for encouraging older people to travel actively, and to take longer journeys on foot in particular. Reducing the speed of traffic - including extra speed bumps and chicanes, lower speed limits in areas where people walk a lot and more crossings to slow traffic down, as well as more speed cameras and better enforcement of speed limit, were suggested to improve safety.


