16 M. Pohle et al.
of the street network or to compare different potential infrastructure design variants. However, since they allow to quantify the risk of street network elements, they can also be used as basis to derive safe routes for different road users and thereby complement common routing options. Within the EU-funded project “SOTERIA”, multiple data driven models especially for vulnerable road users (VRU) are created to identify and quantify numerous independent effects on accident frequency.
1.2 Factors Associated with VRU Accident Frequency
Previous studies have demonstrated that accident frequencies mostly vary by traffic volume. Thereby, studies equally use traffic volumes of motorized traffic as well as of non-motorized traffic. Higher volumes of non-motorized traffic evidently lead to higher accident frequencies of VRUs, however, it must be pointed out that more recent studies show a degressive relationship between both values [1–3]. Studies only regarding the influence of motorized traffic volume commonly describe an increasing effect on VRU accident counts [1] but also opposing effects were found [4] which may be caused by differences in focused study areas. Vehicle speeds seem to reduce VRU accident frequency [5]. However, this effect may only be a proxy for more higher motorized traffic again correlating with a higher design standard for crossing facilities. Concerning infrastructure design, wider streets or with more lanes have been found to increase accident frequency of VRUs [6]. In comparison, mainline medians and crossing medians help to decrease VRU accidents [1]. Furthermore, accident frequencies are lower at signal-controlled sites [6] and at segments with one- or two-sided parking [5]. As socio-demographic influence, a higher population density [3, 4] as well as a high share of both elderly [3, 4] or young people [1, 7] living in the vicinity of streets increased accident frequencies. Regarding land-use influences, commercially used street surroundings [1, 2, 4] as well as heterogenous land-use [7] seem to be linked to higher accident frequencies of VRUs. Analogously, higher crossing density [2, 5] and amount of bus stops [4, 6] in the vicinity increase VRU accidents.
2 Data and Methods
Since characteristics influence accidents of each mode of transport differently or to a different degree, models for each VRU transport mode were created. By way of illustration, in this paper we focus on pedestrian accidents in the State of Saxony, Germany.
2.1 Data
