2.2 Methods for Modelling Accident Frequency
Accident frequency data is usually skewed and discrete making conventional linear regression-based models inappropriate. Poisson-based models have proven to outperform linear regression models, but the underlying distribution consists of only one


18 M. Pohle et al.
parameter “λ” which simultaneously describes the expected value and its variance. However, due to a multitude of factors, the found variance in accident counts likely exceeds this assumption in practice (over dispersion), wherefor negative binomial (NB) are more widely used [10] which allow to model expected value and variance independently. NB-models may, however, may be limited in case of an excess of analysis units with no accidents for which zero-inflated NB-models (ZINB) have been introduced in the past [11]. These link NB-models with logit or probit models. The logit-/ probit-model part accounts for excessing zero counts, whereas remaining counts are modelled by NB-models. This allows the modelling of two different generation processes: with a probability of φk no accidents will be estimated and with a probability of 1- φ k more accidents are estimated by a NB-model. Typical statistical measures were used to evaluate the prediction quality of NB or ZINB as well as their preliminary models during model development, to prevent over-/underfitting and to control for multicollinearity.
