part, segment length was found to be the strongest influence. As part of the zero-inflationmodel part, a negative coefficient for length represents an increase of accidents. During the stepwise model development, it was found that logarithmic length values provided better model results. Segment length also appears as parameter to model higher accident counts. Higher accident counts were found at segments in areas with a high share of industrial/commercial land use which may be a proxy to define higher exposure rates of pedestrian and motorized traffic. Conversely, the proportion of grazing land is likely to describe remote pedestrian facilities with low pedestrian volumes and exposure to motorized traffic resulting in lower accident counts. Relief energy, describing rolling terrain, significantly lowers accident counts and may be due to an unlikely usage of these facilities by pedestrians. As last continuous parameter, road density demonstrates a positive correlation with pedestrian accident counts. It is likely that this parameter, again, highlights areas of high urbanity and thus high pedestrian and motorized traffic flow. The existence of cycling facilities along pedestrian facilities was included as categorical parameter and increased pedestrian accident counts and is plausible with regard to cyclists being the second most common accident opponent of pedestrians. Compared to the baseline of unclassified streets (“other”), higher pedestrian accident counts can be found on classified streets which may describe streets with higher motorized traffic volume and/or wider streets. In comparison, speed limits of at least 50 kph significantly have lower pedestrian accident counts. This unintuitive effect could be explained by speed limit having a direct influence on design standards and thus represent safer pedestrian infrastructure standards. A deficient smoothness on the adjoining road decreased pedestrian accidents counts and vice versa be explained by deficient smoothness preventing higher speeds and thus reducing the risk of an accident. Allowed parking along the street adjoining pedestrian facilities significantly increases pedestrian accident counts and likely describes the effect of a limited visibility of pedestrians between parking cars before crossing streets. Finally, the presence of traffic signals increases the estimated number of pedestrian accidents. However, since traffic signals are installed as a countermeasure to prevent high accident counts, they do not cause more accidents by themselves, but only function as proxy in the model.
4 Outlook
The presented ZINB model does not include influences of traffic volume information yet, but several explanatory parameters which mask effects of exposure both of pedestrians and motorized traffic. Thus, possible derived routing algorithms would more likely represent the subjective perception of safety of road users in the sense that many observed accidents hint on an unsafe area. They, however, do not represent an objective accident risk of a road user. Models in the next project step will include information on trip flows, including volume by road user type, in order to relate accident counts to a personal risk as well as to identify traffic-related explaining factors for accident occurrence. This will enable the development of more accurate predictive models and the definition of objective safety measures.


20 M. Pohle et al.
For this purpose, a travel demand model will be developed which will use anonymized Mobile Network Data (MND) to reconstruct mobility patterns (travel demand). This information will be enriched through machine learning (ML) techniques to overcome the lack of some traveler and journey information. Via aggregation, mobility indicators such as traffic volume per road section and type of VRUs will be generated. Based on the future models with implemented effect of traffic volume, weights can be derived per segment which then truly represent the risk for each type of road and road user and can be used as criteria for a safe route option. This finally allows to develop a ‘safe’ routes planner as part of the SOTERIA project. The proposed routing solution utilizes the aforementioned models using different sub-components:
– routing graph management: dynamical updating of weights on edges – search graph: algorithms to create a least cost path – travel scenarios composition: generation of multi-modal routes – route recommender: provision of route by ‘hard’ (e.g. maximum distance) and ‘soft’ (personalized preferences) constraints
The SOTERIA route planner is being implemented using a micro-services architecture that enables the deployment of the above-described components as individual services. The implementation allows the seamless integration of new data sources and APIs, deployment of the solution in computer clusters for improved performance and operational resilience as well as an easy introduction of additional routing scenarios.
