Oxford Roundabout and the Art of Safety 23
mode share up to 56% [31]. Cycling is inexpensive, eco-friendly, healthy, space-efficient, accessible for many, and may contribute to social inclusion, liveability, sprawl reduction, urban decongestion, noise reduction, economic benefits and city branding [1, 13, 29], and therefore hailed as sustainable, contingent on driving substitution [15]. Monetizing benefits is notoriously difficult, but the societal costs of cars might be six times higher than cycling when these benefits are internalised [12]. Analysing cycling’s integration into an existing transport planning regime is crucial for facilitating sustainable transitions. In most cities, a majority of trips are below 10 kms [17]. E-bicycles extend these distances within daily travel time budgets [18], and better support trips in hilly terrain. Despite this potential, cycling does not happen without nudging and improving safety perceptions, with Copenhagen-style dedicated bicycle facilities being most effective in reducing perceived and objective threats [8, 16, 22, 24]. UK best-practice guidance recommends separated carriageways in high-speed and -traffic environments [30], and is therefore a key policy measure in attracting ‘interested but concerned’ cyclists (i.e. latent demand) and achieving cycling uptake [15, 23]. Resistance to new (cycling) infrastructure stems from automobile-dominant path dependency [28]. Motorists may resist new cycling infrastructure if they perceive it threatens convenience, existing road space, increased congestion, longer travel times, or reduced parking availability, or if they perceive cyclists as a nuisance or risk to traffic safety. Public transport operators may resist competition for road space or ridership. Business owners relying on curbside parking might resist a threat to customer accessibility. Government agencies may resist due to budgetary constraints, allocating funds to ‘higher-priority’ projects. Understanding how cycling infrastructure resistance manifests can inform the policy-making process. This paper examines the barriers, explicit and implicit, to experimenting with cycling infrastructure through a case study of the Plain Roundabout in Oxford, UK. It analyses four aspects through qualitative thematic and discourse analysis of expert interviews: the spatial context within an existing transport network [6, 9, 25], legitimation through policy and cultural discourses [5, 26], institutionalisation potential [5, 20], and the politics and agency [26, 27]. We argue that transitions towards sustainable urban mobility via experimentation face many constraints. Understanding these barriers can inform how abstract concepts of sustainable mobility are adopted by local actors, how (and if) car dominance is challenged in public debate, and whether, by challenging entrenched power and privilege, access to capital and political influence can become more equitable [2].
1.1 The Plain Roundabout
In April 2013, Oxfordshire County Council submitted a bid to improve The Plain roundabout as part of the Cycle City Ambition Grants program [21]. This roundabout, serving as the primary eastern entry into town, had multiple functions but was not performing adequately for any user group. Given that 70% of Oxford’s population resides in East Oxford, The Plain was a critical junction for East Oxford residents’ access to the town centre, with limited alternatives [21]. Despite previous renovations approximately 7 years earlier, The Plain had experienced 35 non-fatal collisions in the previous 5 years,


24 Y. Cornet and H. Hook
marking it as a safety ‘hot spot’ and a potential deterrent to cyclists [21]. The successful bid secured just under £1 million for the roundabout’s renewal. The Plain is an unusual 5-arm roundabout, with two entries (Iffley and Cowley Road) forming a narrow angle to each other [21]. Although the roundabout appears spacious, the central island’s size cannot be reduced due to the presence of an old churchyard and a large tree at its centre. Additionally, facing the Magdalen Bridge entrance, there is a listed Victorian Fountain with a roof, stone columns, and a clock tower.
2 Data and Methods
Thirteen in-depth interviews were conducted with mobility expert stakeholders including representatives from the Oxford Bus Company, community action groups, city and county governments, technical experts, and Oxford University, and one meeting of the community action group was attended to collect cyclist testimonials. Semi-structured interviews were collected between February and May 2015, with additional follow-up interviews in January and February 2016, and lasted approximately 60 min. Participants were recruited by contacting individuals mentioned in the documentation analysis (City Cycling Grant bid material, transport plans, infrastructure guidelines, media articles, and design versions), in the Oxford City Council, and through further snowball sampling. An interview guide was used, consisting of a short introduction, then questioning 1) participant background and role, 2) perspective on the project timeline, 3) controversies and trade-offs, 4) issues emerging in previous interviews, and 5) the data, evidence or KPIs that formed their views and decisions. The semi-structured format allows for flexibility in the questioning approach and provides an ultimately richer dataset to understand the attitudes and opinions of the experts. The interviews were recorded and transcribed by the authors to ensure accuracy. Additional documentation including planning designs, City Cycling Grant bid material, infrastructure guidelines, and media coverage were also included in the analysis. A combination of qualitative thematic and discourse analyses were employed to identify relevant themes and understand the social context of the language used relating to different forms of resistance leading to the failure of this cycling infrastructure experiment. In terms of discourse analysis, patterns of speech, socio-cultural norms, or common references, for example, become the unit of analysis in place of the participants [14]. In other words, as individuals tend to select language that justifies their actions or beliefs, the identification of commonly-used discourses can be useful for understanding their relationships to action. In both types of analysis, inductive coding (or the ‘emic’ approach) was employed, allowing the themes and narratives to be derived from the data instead of predefining the themes [10].
3 Results
Resistance to experimentation with path-breaking change in the Oxford transport regime manifested in four different categories, with 11 sub-themes (Table 1), discussed below. Materiality analysed the physicality of The Plain, and to what extent stakeholders rationalised that this physicality allowed or constrained the possibility for experimenting


