4 Discussion and Conclusion
The case study presented at The Plain offers insight into resistance toward experimentation that challenges historical norms of unfettered motorised movement. Though the arena of automotility of the past has minimised through Oxford’s shift toward buses and cycling tracks, The Plain remains in an arena of high-flow shared space with resistance through Materiality, Institutionalisation, Agency and Discourses preventing a shift toward nearly car-free shared space. This presents a point of decision about the future of mobility - should mode-specific infrastructure be prioritised or should vehicledominance remain unchallenged? As decarbonising transport can be expected to bring more diversification in transport vehicles in the future, deciding what will be allowed to run where is likely to become increasingly relevant. Further, as high-flow shared space does not provide a safe space for movement for all travellers (in fact, road-safety protests have been hosted at The Plain as recently as 30 September 2023 following the release of road death figures [19] and a cycling fatality at The Plain in March 2023), choices must be made about what the goal should be for mobility space and which travellers should be prioritised. In sum, the example of The Plain challenges the notion of experimentation as a sure way to break the status quo and enable change towards more sustainable mobility. Changes toward infrastructure that deprioritize motorised modes will require the questioning of modernist values of speed and unfettered movement. Safety, though touted as an important issue, is not sufficient to justify radical change. Infrastructure interventions are more than just a prioritisation of mode and space, but a product of geographical and material reality, the historical-institutional perspective, and the validity of some discourses against others. These findings can help to provide considerations and information about potential pitfalls in experimentation to policy-makers, ideally improving approaches to new cycling infrastructure interventions.


