Cycling infrastructure, when implemented correctly, increase safety not only for cyclists but also for all other users of public space (Skoczyński, 2021). Since Lisbon was chosen as the city of study, it is important to understand the distribution of cycling infrastructure in the city and their respective kilometers. Lisbon has a good density of cycle paths in some areas, but it also sometimes shows a lack of continuity in these infrastructures and a lack of homogeneity in terms of distribution across the different areas of the city. In order to understand which factors lead to the most severe injuries, the analysis of this study focused on intersections, time of the day, cycling (and non-cycling) infrastructure, and different areas of Lisbon (called clusters). The data used was taken from ANSR (Portuguese National Road Safety Authority) and Câmara Municipal de Lisboa (Lisbon City Hall).
2 Lisbon as a Case Study
Lisbon is a low cycling mature city, which means that it has a very small modal share, to be more precise, it has less than 1% (Félix, 2019). Additionally, Lisbon’s incipient cycle infrastructure is one of the main reasons for its lower modal share of bikes (Moura et al., 2017). Lisbon is known for having high gradients, but 73% of its streets have slopes below 5%, making it an accessible city for most cyclists (Câmara Municipal de Lisboa, 2020). Lisbon has 49% of all cycle infrastructures being protected bike lanes, which means that 49% of all bike lanes offer a physical separation between cyclists and other drivers. The second most common type of bike lane in Lisbon is the mountain bike trail, making up 26.8% of all bike lanes. It’s important to note that this type of cycle path is usually used for leisure rather than for commuting. Figure 1 shows the different types of bike lanes in Lisbon and their distribution across the map. The dark blue line represents protected bike lanes, the dark green line represents mountain bike trails, the red line represents sharrows, the orange line represents buffered bike lanes, and the light green line represents bike lanes shared between pedestrians and cyclists. Most of the mountain bike trails can be found in the Monsanto area, an area characterized by having a dense forest. The protected bike lanes are distributed throughout the city, and alongside the Tagus River (south border of the map). The sharrows are mostly located in the inner areas of the city since they are commonly used in residential areas. The other types of bike lanes are less common, which can also be seen in Fig. 1. Lisbon has 24 districts. To better understand the characteristics of each zone of Lisbon, the 24 districts were grouped into 5 distinct clusters. This grouping was done by joining the most homogeneous districts together, but also by taking geography into account (sharing borders with each other). The districts were grouped as follows:
1. Cluster 1 (in green in Fig. 2) groups Benfica, São Domingos de Benfica, Carnide, Lumiar, and Santa Clara 2. Cluster 2 (in orange) groups Santo António, Arroios, Campolide, Avenidas Novas, Areeiro, and Alvalade


