3 Analysis of Model Results
3.1 Regression’s Overview
For the modeling, two regressions were carried out with good results. One was multinomial logistic regression, and the other was ordinal logistic regression. Although they are similar, they differ in the nature of their dependent variable. Multinomial logistic regression uses a nominal categorical dependent variable, which means that the categories of the variable do not have an inherent order. Ordinal logistic regression uses a categorical ordinal dependent variable, which means that the categories have an inherent order between them. The dependent variable used, and its categories, were the same for both regressions, but in the multinomial, it was considered that there was no order between the categories, while in the ordinal it was considered that there was an inherent order between categories. The dependent variable chosen was injury severity, with 3 categories, Unharmed, Slight injury, and Severe injury. For the ordinal case, it was considered the order of least severe injury (Unharmed) to most severe (Severe injury). For both regressions, 10 independent variables were considered, but for the analysis that follows, the focus will be on just 4 of these 10, as they are the ones that most meet the objective of this study. The 4 independent variables to be analyzed are:
• Intersections – This variable considers whether the accident occurred outside or inside an intersection • Hour – This variable represents the time of day the accident occurred, with 3 categories, Morning, Afternoon, and Night


32 S. de Sousa and L. Picado-Santos
• Cluster – As seen earlier, this variable represents the different areas of Lisbon, and provides information on which cluster the accident occurred in • Bike infrastructure - This variable considers whether the accident occurred outside or inside a bike infrastructure
3.2 Multinomial Logistic Regression
Multinomial logistic regression showed that when compared to slight injuries, accidents that occur at intersections have a higher chance of resulting in severe injuries. Accidents that occur at night also have a higher chance of resulting in severe injuries than slight injuries or even unharmed. As for the different clusters, it was seen that the probability of an accident resulting in severe injury (compared to unharmed) is higher in Cluster 2 than in Cluster 3 and Cluster 4 and 5. The results for bike infrastructure were not significant. Although it was not possible to prove that bike infrastructure has an impact on injury severity, this does not prove that bicycle infrastructure has no impact on injury severity.
3.3 Ordinal Logistic Regression
The ordinal logistic regression showed similar results to the multinomial logistic regression, corroborating the inferences of the multinomial model. Accidents that occur in the evening showed a higher probability of resulting in severe injuries, compared to accidents that occur in the morning. Accidents that occur in Cluster 2 showed a higher probability of resulting in severe injuries than in Cluster 3 and Cluster 4 and 5. The variables intersections and bike infrastructures didn’t show any significant values for this regression, so no conclusion was taken from this part of the analysis.
4 Conclusions
A few conclusions were taken from this study. At night, accidents are more likely to result in serious injuries, possibly due to poor visibility, and excessive alcohol consumption, among other possible reasons. It was also found that accidents at intersections lead to more serious injuries. As for the cycle infrastructure variable, no conclusive results were obtained, since none of the regressions led to statistically significant values. Cluster 2 had a higher chance of severe injuries. This result makes sense given that this is the cluster with the highest number of intersections per km2. On the other hand, this cluster has the highest value of km of cycle infrastructure, which could indicate that it would be safer for cyclists. One reason for the contradictory result could be that there is simply a greater flow of cyclists in this area, which could lead to more accidents and more severe injuries.


Characterization of Accidents with Cyclists in Lisbon 33
With this information, some accident mitigation measures are suggested below:
• Improvement of visibility at night, especially in the cluster 2 zone, and in intersections • Implementation of more bike lanes that offer physical separation. Even though in this analysis it was not possible to fully understand the impact of cycle infrastructures on the injury severity of bicycle accidents, the bike lanes that offer a physical separation are one of the most secure options for cyclists (Cohen, 2013), and the increasing development of bicycle lanes should decrease some dangers of cycling (Skoczyński, 2021) • The implementation of speed bumps (Broach et al., 2012) and sharrows (Skoczyński, 2021) especially in residential zones to not worsen the overall traffic of the city • Implementation of a road safety system that allows for infrastructure treatment of accumulation spots (Carvalheira & Picado-Santos, 2008)
In conclusion, this study shows that there are a few factors that are of paramount importance when dealing with severe injuries of cyclists, and that those factors should be one of the main focus areas when developing mitigation strategies.
