lists road traffic accidents as the leading cause of death in people aged 5–29 years, and experts estimate that 30% of fatal injury crashes are associated with speeding [2, 3]. The relationship between vehicle speed and risk of an accident is exponential. For example, a 2 km/h increase in speed increases the risk of a fatal accident by 20%, while a 10 km/h increase raises the risk by 120% [2, 4]. Similarly, the risk of death increases exponentially. Research from France has shown that that the risk of death of a pedestrian when hit by a car in a frontal collision increases 2 fold between 30 km/h and 40 km/h, and 6 fold between 30 km/h and 50 km/h [5]. This combination of high prevalence and high risk suggests that speeding should be treated as a major public health concern. Speeding is influenced by many factors, including factors related to individuals and factors related to the environment. Individual factors can be related to socio-demographic factors, as well as personality traits, social norms and cultural values [6]. For example, age and gender are known to influence individual speeding behaviour, with 16–24 year olds more likely to speed than older age groups, and males more likely to speed than females [7]. Furthermore, drivers with higher household incomes are more likely to speed than drivers from low household incomes. The personality traits of anger-aggression and impulsive-sensation seeking have also been found to be associated with speeding behaviour [8]. Relatively few studies have investigated environmental factors associated with speeding. These studies used driving simulators [9–11], surveys [12], speed cameras [13] and GPS tracking [14–17], and it was found that a range of environmental factors are related to speeding including lane width, shoulder width, number of lanes, posted speed limit, roadside activity. Speed limit exceedances can be detected using a number of methods, including stationary or mobile cameras, and laser guns. In many countries, the spatial coverage of these methods is limited and only a small fraction of roads can be covered. For example, County Dublin, Ireland, has a road network length of 3950 km, but only 295 camera safety zones. However, citizen science and low-cost sensor networks can help to overcome this limitation by providing additional datasets. The aim of this study is to analyse the influence of environmental factors on the percentage of cars exceeding the speed limit in Dublin based on data collected during a citizen science campaign in 2021.
2 Materials and Methods
2.1 Data Collection and Preparation
WeCount was an EU H2020 project, which used low-cost traffic sensors to measure traffic and car speeds in five case study areas across Europe (Leuven, Barcelona, Cardiff, Ljubljana, Dublin). In Dublin 147 citizens participated in the project. Participants installed a camera based sensor on a window of their house or flat, which overlooked a road. The sensor recorded the number of cars, heavy vehicles, bikes and pedestrians per hour, and it also recorded the frequency distribution of car speeds per hour. Measurements were only collected during daylight hours. Data collected by the sensor was automatically


Spatio-Temporal Analysis of Speed Limit Exceedances 43
uploaded to an open access platform (https://we-count.net/networks/dublin), which displayed hourly counts at the street segment level. Data on this platform is openly accessible and can be downloaded via an API. We downloaded data collected in the Dublin area between 21/03/2021 and 21/09/2021. The low-cost camera based sensors only collected reliable data during daylight; therefore, only hourly data collected between 6:00 and 18:00 h was used. Initially, we used OpenStreetMap to extract the speed limit of each street segment in the WeCount Dublin dataset. However, spot checks indicated errors in the OpenStreetMap speed limits; therefore, the speed limits on all segments were manually verified against GoogleStreetView images. Using the verified speed limit of each road, we calculated the number and percentage of cars driving above the speed limit for each hour and each segment. This data was the outcome variable in our analysis. Potential predictor variables were extracted from several sources of open data, including OpenStreetMap and Met Eireann. The predictor variables used in the analyses can be categorized into spatial variables, temporal variables and spatio-temporal variables, as shown in Table 1.
