4 Discussion and Conclusion
This paper presents findings from a study of the percentage of cars speeding in Dublin based on data collected during a citizen science campaign of 106 street segments. Preliminary analyses of potential predictor variables that only vary spatially indicate that the assigned speed limit of a road is associated with speeding. The proportion of cars speeding is significantly higher on street segments with a 30km/h speed limit than on street segments with a 50km/h or 60km/h speed limit. This is an important finding, because streets with a 30km/h speed limit are often found in residential area, near schools and near shopping areas. These types of streets are likely to have more pedestrians on or near them, which means a larger population is exposed to a higher proportion of speeding cars. In contrast, streets with a speed limit of 50km/h or 60km/h are often larger arterial roads, which pedestrians are more likely to avoid. An association between speeding and the assigned speed limit of a road was also found in a study by Ambros et al. (2020) [14], who found a higher proportion of speeding on roads with a 50km/h limit, compared to roads with a 70km/h or 80km/h limit. We did not find an association between speeding and road features, such as the presence of bus stops or traffic lights, or the road width. Other studies have found that roadside features, such as bus stops, trees, or buildings can reduce speeding [9, 10, 12, 14], and road width in particular has been found to have an influence on speeding [1113]. A possible reason for a lack of association in our study could be the quality the predictor variables, as they were extracted from OpenStreetMap data. If a more accurate and detailed dataset was available of the street layout at each segment, these analytical results may change. The total number of cars exceeding the speed limit during the six month time period was 3,062,608 (Table 2). The current fixed penalty charge in Ireland for exceeding the speed limit is e160 and 3 points on the driver’s license. Therefore, the speed limit exceedances measured during this study would have been equivalent to penalty charges of e490 million and 9.2 million points. On 4th September 2023 the Irish police (An Garda Síochána) held a National Slow Down Day with speed checks by both Garda members and GoSafe Safety Cameras. Over 24 h the speeds of 149,488 vehicles were checked, and 865 cars were recorded as speeding [22], which is equivalent to 0.6%. Our study measured a much higher proportion of speeding (7.4%), and given the data from previous road users’ surveys [1], we would expect that 7.4% underestimates the true value. Based on newspaper reports, the National Slow Down Day seemed to focus on motorways, national roads and regional roads with high speed limits, which may explain the low percentage of speeding. Based on the preliminary findings of our research, we would recommend to deploy more speed cameras on local roads with low speed limits as the proportion of speeding is higher on these roads and a larger population is at risk.


