Abstract. UN Regulation No. 157 was the first to establish worldwide uniform provisions for the type-approval of SAE Level 3 (L3) Automated Driving Systems (ADS) called Automated Lane Keeping Systems (ALKS). To ensure the safety of the systems, detailed test provisions were introduced, which include mandatory public road tests. The present work is focusing on public road testing and safety assessment of ALKS systems and reports on the methodology, challenges and lessons learned from a proof-of-concept experimental campaign. In line with the regulatory requirements concerning the minimum duration of system verification on public roads, various traffic circumstances and mandatory traffic scenarios were all considered in carrying out the experimental campaign. Since there was no suitable ADS on the market, a vehicle equipped with an SAE L2 system was used instead. The effort was aimed at providing a practical proof of concept for the regulatory provisions and not verifying the compliance of the vehicle under test. By carefully planning test execution, the authors managed to encounter the required traffic density conditions in all needed traffic scenarios with their necessary repetition number, within four testing days. In conclusion, the experimental campaign put in place under UN R157 proved to be a feasible exercise.
Keywords: Automated Vehicles Testing · Motorway Driving · Measurement Campaign · Policy Support · UN Regulation No. 157
1 Introduction
Driving automation is witnessing a progressive transition from a dream to reality, featuring different systems being type-approved across the world [1]. A major step forward was undertaken with the United Nations Economic Commission for Europe (UNECE) Regulation No. 157 (UN R157, [2]). UN R157 was the first regulation to establish worldwide harmonized provisions for the type-approval of an Automated Driving System (ADS) named Automated Lane Keeping System (ALKS). According to the scope of the regulation, the system can only be activated on highways. The maximum operational speed is restricted to 130 km/h and automated
© The Author(s) 2026 C. McNally et al. (Eds.): TRAconference 2024, LNMOB, pp. 56–61, 2026. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-88974-5_9


Public Road Tests of Automated Lane Keeping Systems 57
lane changes are allowed. When it is active, the responsibility of vehicle control and monitoring the environment is entirely on the ADS. Test provisions were introduced to reflect the level of risks associated with the usual speeds of highway driving, creating dedicated parts for track testing (Annex 5 of the regulation) and public road testing (Annex 6). According to the latter, a minimum duration of 15 operating hours shall be recorded on public roads with the system activated. From these 15 h, at least 5 h should be devoted to heavily congested traffic conditions. The residual 10 h should cover free-flow traffic conditions, if applicable according to the vehicle’s Operational Design Domain (ODD). An important exemption mentioned in the annex is regarding measurement time. According to this, if the testing exercise was continued for at least 16 h, i.e. 2 working days of testing (not to be confused with the requirement for 15 recorded operational hours), the authority may decide to abandon the tests. In this case, the manufacturer should provide the remaining test scenarios from internal system validation results. Nonetheless, a set of test scenarios should be recorded during public road operation, the list of these is included in Annex 6. It has to be pointed out that within 15 h of road driving it is unlikely to meet critical traffic scenes. The public road tests can only provide an indication that the behavior of the vehicle under test (VUT) is acceptable under typical traffic conditions. The current contribution summarizes the preliminary findings of a public road test carried out according to UN R157. The goal is to provide guidance and recommendations for obtaining valid, significant and traceable results as well as suitable equipment for such type approval tests.
2 Methodology and Case Study
Public road testing is the final step in the type approval process. The safety assurance process of the manufacturer has first been evaluated by the safety audit, while the general performance of safety-relevant functions has been demonstrated in the documentation by simulations and internal validation tests performed by the manufacturer. In addition, safety-critical reference scenarios have been verified in proving ground tests. The purpose of the real-road test is to provide evidence concerning the driving behavior of the ADS in real traffic and under changing environmental conditions. To support the assessment of an ADS, the present contribution summarizes how the road test concept has been designed and performed.
2.1 Experimental Campaign
The experimental campaign was planned to suit regulation requirements as effectively as possible from the perspective of the needed duration. The test drive routes were selected based on usual traffic conditions on a given section of road at a given time. Three days of tests were foreseen to cover the required heavily congested and free flow traffic. Time of day was also considered during the planning, e.g. traffic jam measurements were performed during early morning rush-hours. By the end of the three-day measurement campaign additional heavily congested driving recordings still had to be taken. The missing amount of measurements were performed, by an additional half-day test drive.


58 S. Vass et al.
There was no suitable automated vehicle on the market at the time of the test campaign. Therefore, the authors decided to carry out the assessment using a vehicle equipped with Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS), i.e. Adaptive Cruise Control (ACC) and Lane Keeping System (LKS). This fact did not have effects on the results, since the focus of the current work is on the validation of the technical feasibility of the test provisions and not on the compliance of the vehicle itself. The ADAS functionalities were used to simulate ALKS capabilities for all in-lane driving. Necessary lane changes (including overtakes, entering and exiting highways, merging, etc.) and speed settings according to the speed limit signs were the task of the human driver. The campaign was carried out using a wide range of measurement tools. The instrumentation included a high accuracy positioning system together with four cameras facing in the four directions and three LiDARs for the detection of traffic participants. Most of the perception algorithms used to generate the metadata are low-level fusion algorithms. In this type of fusion, more accurate and robust information can be extracted by combining sensor data. Some of the applied algorithms use only camera images incorporating homography or only LiDAR point clouds with various machine learning algorithms. Perception algorithms included traffic rule recognition (e.g. traffic sign identification), road parameter determination (e.g. lane width, road curvature radii, lane marking) and detection of other road users.
2.2 Requirements of UN R157
UN R157 reports provisions for the ALKS vehicle to maintain a safe following distance with respect to the vehicle ahead. This should be tested with different types of lead vehicles, including passenger cars and heavy-duty vehicles. For speeds up to 60 km/h explicit threshold are defined, above this speed local traffic rules shall be considered. Lane keeping functionality should be tested with different curvature radii and speeds to ensure that the ALKS vehicle maintains a stable position within the lane in various circumstances. UN R157 includes explicit requirements for lane changes. These ensure that the ALKS does not disrupt traffic and that the maneuver is sufficiently smooth and properly signaled to the other traffic participants. Critical cut-in scenarios are statistically unlikely to happen within 15 h highway driving, therefore public road tests of UN R157 require to include only scenarios similar to cut-ins: different merging situations or another vehicle changing lane close in front of the ALKS vehicle. Repetition of certain merging scenarios is required. Additional scenarios that should be encountered during the testing include: system override by the driver, traffic rule compliance and different road events (e.g. tunnels, work zones, changing environmental conditions, etc.).
