In the sub-sample of data, the VUT has been driving without interaction with other road users 58% of the time, meaning that either no traffic participant was in front of the VUT or cases where a vehicle was more than 3 s ahead of the VUT. This corresponds to the definition in Annex 6 of the regulation. No instance of heavily congested traffic was recorded in the considered subsample and the VUT was travelling under lightly congested conditions for 5% of the time. These considerations find a graphical evidence in Fig. 1, where a clear spike indicates that the VUT travelled at the ACC set speed for the relative majority of the time. The following distance requirement was evaluated by extracting cases where the VUT speed was stationary for 5 s and during which a lead vehicle affected the behavior of the VUT. The stationarity requirement is needed to ensure that only a stable car-following regime is part of the analysis. The resultant sample points are shown in Fig. 2 together with the 1 s and 2 s following rules. Overall, the ACC uses a lower headway than recommended by the national traffic law in Hungary. Here it has to be noted that the ACC is a driver assistance system, where the driver can set the following time-gap and also the target speed. The following time-gap policy of the VUT was similar to the time-gap applied by other road users and the VUT occupants also judged it to be sufficiently safe. The ACC of the VUT during the whole measurement campaign was set to “medium” following time-gap.
4 Lessons Learned
Testing automated vehicles with substantial ODD limitations requires careful preparations in order to meet the prescribed traffic conditions. As an example, selecting busy highways during the morning or evening rush hour enabled the recording of the 5 h


60 S. Vass et al.
Fig. 2. Following distance versus 1 s and 2 s rules.
of congested traffic situations. On the other hand, selecting highways known to be less busy, and executing the test drive during the day made the collection of free-flow hours relatively easy. The data collection for the 15 h of operation took altogether 3.5 days, including all preparatory works (starting up the systems, cleaning sensors, filling up the VUT, etc.) and data handling activities after returning from the tests. During the test campaign, more than 1500 km were covered and all the mandatory scenario types were encountered with the necessary repetition number. No inclement weather was met, and light conditions were limited only to daylight circumstances. Depending on the ODD of a given ALKS system, these have to be considered as additional factors for the planning of the measurements. The measurement equipment of the VUT is a complex and expensive system. Such a system needs to be operated and evaluated by skilled personnel. Vehicle adaption, calibration, and evaluation of the traffic scene in total require a considerable amount of time, expertise and a substantial financial effort. The result of the entire exercise is a dataset with the traffic situations as seen by the independent reference system mounted on the vehicle. As such, the collected data does not contain the information used by the ADS for the operation, therefore one cannot easily assess the environment-sensing system of the VUT. This is enough to evaluate compliance with the regulation but does not allow for a deep safety assessment of the system. For the evaluation, it is not necessary to have an independent perception system, more effective would be to use the vehicle’s own perception system. Eventually, we have to note that the amount of raw data generated by the cameras and LiDARs is several orders higher than the metadata derived from it and used for the safety assessment. Recording only the necessary signals calculated by the VUT is a considerably easier task.


Public Road Tests of Automated Lane Keeping Systems 61
5 Conclusion
A test campaign was aimed at investigating the feasibility of the requirements mandated by UN R157 for the type-approval of ALKS: the first road-legal ADS. The experimental contribution leveraged a vehicle with ADAS due to the unavailability of an ALKSfeatured vehicle. The VUT was equipped with a state-of-the-art measurement system to provide the lists of signals mandated by the regulation and to assess the vehicle’s technical requirements compliance with respect to the provisions. The measurement system used is capable of providing the necessary signals but requires a substantial financial and time investment, calibration and data extraction effort. A more efficient candidate with further advantages would be to use the perception system of the VUT itself provided that the system has been validated beforehand. The preliminary findings suggest that UN R157 requirements can be reliably verified if the data collection procedure is accurately planned and the measurements are sufficiently robust. Selection of the routes according to the usual traffic density on different calendar days (e.g. weekdays or weekends) and time of day substantially contributed to covering all necessary scenarios and time provisions of the regulation within 4 days. The non-compliances reported from our test campaign shall not be interpreted as deficiencies of the VUT’s set of ADAS. Instead, they are representative of the measurement system’s capability to grasp the failure to achieve compliance with the technical requirements.
