Abstract. Effects of rear-light usage by daylight were examined. A field experiment measured passing vehicles’ lateral distance to a stationary car, as an effect of rear-light status. A questionnaire survey measured car drivers’ perceptions regarding safety and visibility depending on rear-light usage, and their experiences, preferences, and beliefs about own usage of rear lights in daylight. An observational study measured percentages of cars with lit and unlit rear lights, respectively, in sunshine during summer. Results show that rear lights give greater lateral distance when being passed by. A car with lit rear lights is interpreted as more likely to start driving. Lit rear lights are preferred and estimated to give better visibility and traffic safety. Only a small proportion of drivers know that they drive with unlit rear lights in daylight, whilst the majority actually drive with unlit rear lights. Conclusion: lit rear lights in daylight improves traffic safety. It is therefore recommended that rear lights with sufficient brightness to be seen even in sunshine should be legally required.
Keywords: Rear light · taillight · tail light · vehicle lighting · daytime running lights · traffic safety · visibility · detectability · road user behaviour · driver behaviour
1 Introduction
This study investigated the effects of car rear lights by daylight, dawn and dusk – in light conditions when cars should be clearly visible even without lit headlights and rear lights. In daylight, lit rear lights should not contribute much and significantly to whether cars are detected, or how early or at what distance they are detected. However, it is conceivable that if a car does not have its rear lights visibly lit, it may be more difficult for other drivers to identify if it is running, and if it can therefore make a sudden change in position. A car with lit rear lights is reasonably more likely to have a person in the driver’s seat, and its engine should be more likely to be running. To avoid collisions, it can be time-critical to detect vehicles in traffic, where they are, in which direction they are traveling, and what their drivers intend to do. A vehicle must have the lights and headlights that are prescribed for the vehicle lit, however, if prescribed warning lights are used on a car in daylight, the rear or side lights do not need to be lit [1].
