In 2011, the European Union introduced requirements for automatic daytime running lights at the front of all new cars, but in order to reduce carbon dioxide emissions, this did not apply to the rear lights [2]. A requirement was introduced in August 2016 that new passenger cars and trucks must also have automatic rear lights, at minimum when the ambient brightness falls below 1000 lx, for example in darkness or when driving in tunnels [2, 3]. It has been claimed that rear lights can be unlit without impairing road safety and that unlit rear lights are a way to save energy and for the car industry to work towards more sustainable driving (e.g., [4]), and that there are no studies that indicate that unlit rear lights during the day would reduce road safety and that lit rear lights rather impedes the visibility of the brake lights (e.g., [3], citing [5]). However, [5] is both 20 years old and lacks reference to such empirical evidence. Rear lights on cars also vary widely in brightness, and many newer versions are dimly lit. When the sun shines on a newer car, it can therefore be very difficult to see if its rear lights are lit. At the same time, it is quite clearly visible if the same car’s headlights are on, because the headlights shine significantly brighter. Previous Swedish large-scale observational surveys showed that 15% of vehicles had unlit rear lights in the dark in 2018, and that 5% had unlit rear lights in the dark while 21% had unlit rear lights in daylight in 2021 [6, 7]. The overall aim was to investigate the effects of rear-light status (i.e., whether the rear lights are lit or unlit) on driver behavior in surrounding traffic; and drivers’ attitudes and experiences of visibility and road safety as an effect of rear-light status. In addition, the agreement between Swedish drivers’ awareness of whether they have their rear lights lit or unlit in daylight, and the actual use of rear lights in daylight was also investigated. The study was conducted as a field experiment, a questionnaire survey, and an observational study.
2 Field Experiment
The purpose was to investigate whether a stationary vehicle’s rear-light status (lit/unlit) affects how much lateral distance passing vehicles keep. A further aim was to investigate what percentage of Swedish passenger cars have unlit rear lights at sunrise, sunset and during the day.
Method. Data was collected on five different occasions in April 2023 on a country road with single lanes in both directions. The speed limit is 70 km/h, lane width 3.5 m, dashed center line (3 m long, 9 m interval), and dashed edge line (1 m long, 1.5 m interval). Two occasions were at dawn, two at dusk, and one was a gray overcast morning. A grey Toyota Avensis station wagon, 2010 model, rear lights with bulbs, filament, was parked in the road clearing, 1 dm to the right of the dashed border. Inside were three cameras, two of which were directed backwards, and one forward. The rear lights were lit and unlit approximately every four minutes, whenever possible when no vehicle was approaching and could see the lights being switched on or off. N = 850 passing vehicles were videotaped, n = 765 related to their own rear-light status (on or off). Neither drivers nor license plates can be identified on the video recordings. The lateral position on the road surface (the position of the vehicle on the road surface laterally) was measured


Effects of Rear Lights on Cars by Daylight 71
from video recordings of the lateral position of the vehicle’s right front tire on the road approximately 5 m before it passed the test car.
Results. When the stationary test car had its rear lights lit, the average lateral position of the passing vehicles was M = 5.48 cm (SD = 1.46 cm); with rear lights unlit, the average lateral position was M = 5.20 cm (SD = 1.46 cm), t(848) = 2.33, p < .05, d = 0.16. Converted from measurement on the monitor to reality (on the road surface), the average lateral position was about 9 cm further to the left when the test car’s rear lights were lit. At sunrise, n = 169 (28%) of passing vehicles had unlit rear lights, while at sunset, n = 19 (23%) had unlit rear lights. During the day, n = 48 (59%) had their rear lights off.
3 Questionnaire Survey
The purpose was to investigate why passing vehicles pass laterally farther from a stationary vehicle with lit rear lights. In addition, car drivers’ attitudes to lit and unlit rear lights in daylight, and the knowledge of current legislation on the use of rear lights, were investigated.
Method. Participants for the survey study were recruited via advertisement on social media 11–17 May 2023 and via VTI’s own website, aimed at users aged 18 and over, throughout Sweden. In addition, the Swedish Homeowners Association advertised on 22 May 2023 via their monthly member email to approximately 19,000 members. In the opening text of the questionnaire, it was stated that the person who would participate in the study needed to have a valid passenger car driver’s license. The social media advertisement reached 52,480 users and was shown an average of 1.87 times per user. N = 9282 respondents aged 18–100 years (M = 60 years 4 months, SD = 12 years 7 months) answered the survey, of which 30.4% women, 69.0% men and 0,5% who did not want to state gender, from all of Sweden’s counties. The respondents were randomly allocated to the four questionnaire versions via a web link on VTI’s website. For between-group comparisons, video and still images of the test car were shown with either the rear lights lit, or unlit. For within-group comparison, video and still images were shown where the test car had the rear lights both lit and unlit, and in two different presentation orders. In all questionnaire versions, video was shown without sound, filmed from inside a car that passed the test car. The video was recorded on a test track without other traffic, in a right curve so that the test car was not visible from the beginning. The purpose of the design was to first examine how the respondents pay attention to and estimate overtaking when they were unaware that the study is about a difference between lit and unlit rear lights, and to also (with within-group comparison) examine how they assess the difference depending on whether the rear lights were on or not. The purpose of the final block of questions was to investigate attitudes, how they think they use their rear lights themselves, and the level of knowledge regarding the use of rear lights. For all estimates of visibility, clarity, and risk, 101-point (0–100) visual analog scales were used, with 50 as the midpoint (e.g., no difference between video clips). The direction of the rating scales was consistent with the order of the video clips, so that the order of presentation with rear light lit and unlit was reflected in the rating


