4 Observational Study
In order to map more exhaustively the proportion of passenger cars and trucks in categories M1 and N1 that are driven without visibly lit rear lights in daylight, and in sunny weather during summertime, surrounding traffic was observed during car journeys in southern Sweden on 18 July 2023 and 22 July 2023 on the E4 motorway between Mantorp and Ljungby, and 28 July 2023 on the E22 between Kristianstad and Sölvesborg. Of the N = 794 cars, n = 143 (18%) clearly had lit rear lights, while for n = 49 cars (6%) it could not be judged whether the rear lights were on or whether it was the lamp glass that retroreflected the sunlight, and n = 602 cars (76%) clearly appeared to have unlit rear lights. The percentage of cars that did not have rear lights clearly switched on was thus 82% (n = 651 cars).


74 B. Lidestam et al.
5 Discussion
The majority of Swedish drivers do not have lit rear lights by daylight, although they think they do. A large percentage stated that they do not know what the law says about rear lights during the day. The vast majority also prefer that surrounding traffic should have their rear lights on, because they think that the vehicles are then more clearly visible, that it is safer, and that it is easier to determine whether the vehicles are moving – which, for example, increases the perceived likelihood that a vehicle may suddenly start driving when you are about to pass it. This is also reflected in that drivers keep a greater distance laterally when passing a stationary car with lit rear lights. Lit rear lights on vehicles, in daylight, is therefore highly desirable from a trafficsafety point of view. The recommendation is that the current EU legislation on the use of rear lights be reviewed and revised, to make it mandatory to have lit rear lights with sufficient brightness to be seen even in sunny weather.
